Situation: A suspicious file was found on a user's machine that needs immediate review. The EDR has already released multiple alerts. 
I have to analyse the file, collect anything to identify it, gather any potential IOCs, correlate and analyse the alerts for potential malicious behaviour. 

Note: I was given a lot of built in tools and some I was not sure what their use was so I had do some research here.

The first couple of questions was just doing analysis on the windows binary. Upon research, I found pestudio, a static analysis tool use for executablesw (without running them).

The tool returned a report and I was quickly able to find the SHA256 hash of the file as well as its architecture (x64bit). A hint that I was given was that the URL within the file is an IOC. 
After going into the indicators section of the results, I was able to find it. To find the domain, I ran a find strings command using the information from the URL. It also returned a string of text.

After decoding it, using cyberchef from base 64, I was able to get the flag for the first task.

The last question in this task was to find the library used by the binary for socket communication. Going back to pestudio in the libraries section, I looked through all the results until I found one referencing socket communication.

The 2nd task required using a static analysis website. The entries imitate alerts from EDR systems. My first two steps was analyzing the urls from each flag. Looking at the commands for the first flag, I could see some the lines Frombase64(). I copied the text inside the parenthesis into cyberchef, and I was able to retrieve the URL's.

The 2nd flag was actually in a format I did not immediately recognize. After looking it up, I learned they were charcode values. I was still able to decode them using the decimal mode in Cyberchef.

The powershell command from the second alert also shared the file name that was saved.

This challenge was another short one but I really liked. I got experience with pestudio, an incredible tool that I had never used before.

